#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79e281dd-40c0-515a-a5d5-d063609ecf73" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals of Unity With Respect to X"}] :question/id #uuid "30d54dcc-49a2-5cd0-9eb9-659413ed5c67" :question/math-academy-id "e-9525" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d x$, where $C=C_1 \\cup C_2$ is the path comprising of two line segments, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3708/Source/Images/8e812447b89c4e3aa73cf9f3313ab5fc.png)" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d x = x_2 - x_1\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and $x_1$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, $x_2 = -3$ and $x_1 = 3$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d x = x_2 - x_1 = (-3) - 3 = -6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6935944-0acd-5593-aa80-e56f6014c13f" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals of Unity With Respect to Y"}] :question/id #uuid "d799bcde-dbf0-580e-921b-18e56d729ea1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9526" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d y$, where $C$ is the path along the semicircle of radius $3$, traversed from the point $(3,0)$ to the point $(-3,0)$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3708/Source/Images/57e461e49aa9411e4aeb899975abcd92.png)" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d y = y_2 - y_1\n$$\n\nwhere $y_2$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and $y_1$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, $y_2 = 0$ and $y_1 = 0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d y = y_2 - y_1 = 0 - 0 = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fd19952-77af-5f27-a7ef-adf46b1958ae" :knowledge-point/title "Orientation-Dependence"}] :question/id #uuid "e1d24f1c-4b7c-5c5e-bf93-7f9e76dffdf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9527" :question/problem "If $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d x= 8$ and $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d x= -2$, calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d x$ given that $C = C_1\\cup C_2$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nC = C_1\\cup C_2\n$$\n\nbe a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation.\n\nFor line integrals with respect to $x$, we have the following properties:\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = -\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x$\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x + \\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x$\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C}f(x, y)dx &= ∫_{C_{1}}f(x, y)dx + ∫_{C_{2}}f(x, y)dx \\\\\n8 &= - ∫_{-C_{1}}f(x, y)dx - ∫_{-C_{2}}f(x, y)dx \\\\\n8 &= - (-2) - ∫_{-C_{2}}f(x, y)dx \\\\\n8 &= 2 - ∫_{-C_{2}}f(x, y)dx \\\\\n6 &= - ∫_{-C_{2}}f(x, y)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d x = -6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79e281dd-40c0-515a-a5d5-d063609ecf73" :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals of Unity With Respect to X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b789fde0-58a5-5c55-b956-e0057d4c28b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177581} {:answer/id #uuid "d7406eac-1069-562a-9b51-334a25b31658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177582} {:answer/id #uuid "459a4552-03f1-5246-a238-cf75